1
00:00:00,378 --> 00:00:04,545
Last time, we ran one short algorithm on a graph.

2
00:00:04,545 --> 00:00:06,589
Take a token from the pile.

3
00:00:06,589 --> 00:00:08,777
If its vertex is new, mark it.

4
00:00:08,777 --> 00:00:12,527
Then add a token for every edge leaving that vertex.

5
00:00:12,527 --> 00:00:14,662
Today we ask two questions.

6
00:00:14,662 --> 00:00:16,537
How long does that take?

7
00:00:16,537 --> 00:00:19,111
And what happens if we change the pile?

8
00:00:20,020 --> 00:00:22,689
Here is a city metro map.

9
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:27,702
Each station is a vertex, and each track between two stations is an edge.

10
00:00:27,702 --> 00:00:29,421
You begin at Arch.

11
00:00:29,421 --> 00:00:33,158
A station counts as visited the first time you arrive there.

12
00:00:33,158 --> 00:00:36,817
Every track you could still try is a token: from which station,

13
00:00:36,817 --> 00:00:37,859
to which.

14
00:00:37,859 --> 00:00:41,319
And the station you arrived from is that station's parent.

15
00:00:42,217 --> 00:00:44,691
Before we count anything, guess.

16
00:00:44,691 --> 00:00:46,826
The map has twelve tracks.

17
00:00:46,826 --> 00:00:51,901
How many tokens get put in the pile in total,
counting every track you could ever try?

18
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,652
Look at one track, between Arch and Bay.

19
00:01:01,652 --> 00:01:06,001
When you arrive at Arch, you add a token to try this track toward Bay.

20
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:10,350
When you arrive at Bay, you add a token to try it back toward Arch.

21
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:14,747
So every track makes two tokens, one from each end.

22
00:01:15,630 --> 00:01:18,638
Do that for all twelve tracks, one after another,

23
00:01:18,638 --> 00:01:20,678
and we get twenty-four tokens.

24
00:01:21,609 --> 00:01:24,304
Add the one token we start with, at Arch.

25
00:01:24,304 --> 00:01:26,401
Twenty-five tokens in all.

26
00:01:26,401 --> 00:01:28,992
Two times E, plus one.

27
00:01:28,992 --> 00:01:32,651
Two times twelve, plus one, is twenty-five.

28
00:01:32,651 --> 00:01:35,238
That is exactly the count from last time.

29
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:40,226
Every token is put in the pile once, and taken out once.

30
00:01:40,226 --> 00:01:43,950
Call the time for one put, or one take, T.

31
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:48,217
Then all the tokens together cost about two E times T.

32
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:54,146
Each station is also marked only once, which adds about V more work.

33
00:01:54,146 --> 00:01:57,688
So the total time is V plus E times T.

34
00:01:57,688 --> 00:01:59,446
The V is the marking.

35
00:01:59,446 --> 00:02:02,085
The E times T is the tokens.

36
00:02:02,991 --> 00:02:07,119
With a stack, a put or a take only touches the top of the pile,

37
00:02:07,119 --> 00:02:10,491
so T is a constant, however big the pile gets.

38
00:02:10,491 --> 00:02:12,770
The total is V plus E.

39
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:14,745
We call that linear time.

40
00:02:15,634 --> 00:02:16,832
One warning.

41
00:02:16,832 --> 00:02:21,168
The book assumes the map is stored as a list of neighbors for each station.

42
00:02:21,168 --> 00:02:24,540
If it were stored as a table of yes or no answers,

43
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:28,577
finding one station's neighbors would mean checking every cell in a row,

44
00:02:28,577 --> 00:02:33,104
V steps each time, and the total would grow like V times V.

45
00:02:34,019 --> 00:02:35,425
Now the pile.

46
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:39,735
The proof only needed somewhere to put tokens in and take tokens out.

47
00:02:39,735 --> 00:02:42,027
It never said which one to take.

48
00:02:42,027 --> 00:02:45,608
Here is one pile with four numbered tokens.

49
00:02:45,608 --> 00:02:47,925
Take the newest, and that is a stack.

50
00:02:47,925 --> 00:02:50,399
Take the oldest, and that is a queue.

51
00:02:50,399 --> 00:02:53,937
Take the smallest number, and that is a priority queue.

52
00:02:54,835 --> 00:02:57,674
Same algorithm, three behaviors.

53
00:02:57,674 --> 00:03:00,070
A stack gives depth-first search.

54
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:02,517
A queue gives breadth-first search.

55
00:03:02,517 --> 00:03:05,473
A priority queue gives best-first search.

56
00:03:05,473 --> 00:03:08,373
Let us run the first two on the metro map.

57
00:03:09,336 --> 00:03:10,860
First, the stack.

58
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:13,736
The newest token always goes first.

59
00:03:13,736 --> 00:03:15,599
Watch how deep it goes.

60
00:03:15,599 --> 00:03:19,375
From Arch, ride to Dock, then Fort, then Gate, then East,

61
00:03:19,375 --> 00:03:22,184
then Cove, and last of all, Bay.

62
00:03:28,271 --> 00:03:31,735
The came-from links form one long, skinny chain,

63
00:03:31,735 --> 00:03:33,232
six tracks deep.

64
00:03:33,232 --> 00:03:38,805
Bay is right next to Arch, yet in this tree,
the route to Bay passes through five other

65
00:03:38,805 --> 00:03:39,947
stations.

66
00:03:40,844 --> 00:03:42,198
Now the queue.

67
00:03:42,198 --> 00:03:45,388
From Arch, three tokens are in the pile.

68
00:03:45,388 --> 00:03:47,874
This time we take the oldest token.

69
00:03:47,874 --> 00:03:49,954
Which one comes out first?

70
00:03:54,862 --> 00:03:56,867
Arch to Bay, the oldest.

71
00:03:56,867 --> 00:03:58,026
Bay is new.

72
00:03:58,026 --> 00:04:00,722
Then Arch to Cove, Cove is new.

73
00:04:00,722 --> 00:04:03,195
Then Arch to Dock, Dock is new.

74
00:04:03,195 --> 00:04:06,985
Every station next to Arch is reached before we go any further.

75
00:04:06,985 --> 00:04:08,817
Those are one stop away.

76
00:04:09,741 --> 00:04:12,853
Now the tokens from Bay, Cove, and Dock.

77
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:16,629
Most lead to stations already visited, so they are thrown away.

78
00:04:16,629 --> 00:04:18,908
Bay to East: East is new.

79
00:04:18,908 --> 00:04:21,759
Most of Cove's tokens are thrown away.

80
00:04:21,759 --> 00:04:24,572
But Cove to Fort: Fort is new.

81
00:04:24,572 --> 00:04:27,641
East and Fort are two stops from Arch.

82
00:04:28,541 --> 00:04:31,718
Next come Dock's tokens, and then East's.

83
00:04:31,718 --> 00:04:33,255
Most are thrown away.

84
00:04:33,255 --> 00:04:37,799
At last, East to Gate: Gate is new, the final station.

85
00:04:37,799 --> 00:04:39,179
Three stops.

86
00:04:39,179 --> 00:04:42,300
Everything left in the pile is thrown away.

87
00:04:43,204 --> 00:04:45,730
The queue gives a short, bushy tree.

88
00:04:45,730 --> 00:04:48,543
Every station is at most three stops from Arch.

89
00:04:48,543 --> 00:04:52,215
Put it next to the stack's tree, and the difference is clear:

90
00:04:52,215 --> 00:04:54,867
depth six, against depth three.

91
00:04:55,779 --> 00:04:57,446
That is no accident.

92
00:04:57,446 --> 00:05:02,016
Each round reaches one stop further, so the
number of stops at which a station is first

93
00:05:02,016 --> 00:05:04,907
reached is the fewest tracks between it and Arch.

94
00:05:04,907 --> 00:05:07,003
Bay: one stop.

95
00:05:07,003 --> 00:05:10,154
Gate: three stops, and no route is shorter.

96
00:05:10,154 --> 00:05:13,735
Breadth-first search finds the routes with the fewest stops.

97
00:05:13,735 --> 00:05:15,697
Depth-first search does not.

98
00:05:16,621 --> 00:05:20,176
The third rule puts a number on every track, say,

99
00:05:20,176 --> 00:05:23,652
the minutes of travel, and always takes the smallest.

100
00:05:23,652 --> 00:05:25,371
That is best-first search.

101
00:05:25,371 --> 00:05:29,720
With the track times as the numbers, it builds a minimum spanning tree:

102
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:33,379
that is Jarník's algorithm, which most people call Prim's.

103
00:05:33,379 --> 00:05:38,418
With the time so far as the number, it finds
the fastest routes on a weighted map:

104
00:05:38,418 --> 00:05:40,514
that is Dijkstra's algorithm.

105
00:05:40,514 --> 00:05:46,218
A priority queue costs a little more per token, so the time is V plus E log E.

106
00:05:46,218 --> 00:05:48,571
Later chapters return to both.

107
00:05:49,474 --> 00:05:50,906
A new problem.

108
00:05:50,906 --> 00:05:55,177
Across the water, a separate ferry network has three stations:

109
00:05:55,177 --> 00:05:57,404
Mill, Nest, and Oak.

110
00:05:57,404 --> 00:05:59,630
They connect only to each other.

111
00:05:59,630 --> 00:06:04,214
Your search from Arch visits the whole metro, and the pile empties.

112
00:06:04,214 --> 00:06:05,711
The search stops.

113
00:06:05,711 --> 00:06:09,002
Mill, Nest, and Oak are never visited.

114
00:06:09,934 --> 00:06:12,265
To reach everywhere, add a loop.

115
00:06:12,265 --> 00:06:14,413
Go through every station.

116
00:06:14,413 --> 00:06:19,140
If a station has not been visited yet, it belongs to a new network.

117
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:22,118
Count it, and start a fresh search from there.

118
00:06:23,022 --> 00:06:24,272
In Python.

119
00:06:24,272 --> 00:06:28,100
A set called visited remembers which stations we have seen.

120
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:30,145
Networks starts at zero.

121
00:06:30,145 --> 00:06:33,751
For each station in the graph, if the station is not in visited,

122
00:06:33,751 --> 00:06:38,230
we found a new network, so add one, and run the search from that station,

123
00:06:38,230 --> 00:06:39,815
which fills in visited.

124
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:41,593
Run it.

125
00:06:41,593 --> 00:06:45,343
Arch starts network one, and marks seven stations.

126
00:06:45,343 --> 00:06:48,481
Mill starts network two, and marks three.

127
00:06:48,481 --> 00:06:49,757
Two networks.

128
00:06:49,757 --> 00:06:53,389
Every vertex and every edge is still visited only once,

129
00:06:53,389 --> 00:06:56,671
so even with the loop, it is still V plus E.

130
00:06:56,671 --> 00:06:59,623
The same loop counts the networks for free.

131
00:07:00,517 --> 00:07:02,666
Now make some tracks one-way.

132
00:07:02,666 --> 00:07:07,223
An arrow from Arch to Bay means trains only run from Arch to Bay.

133
00:07:07,223 --> 00:07:09,827
The search code does not change at all.

134
00:07:09,827 --> 00:07:13,756
A marked station just adds a token for each arrow leaving it.

135
00:07:14,626 --> 00:07:15,537
Run it from Arch.

136
00:07:15,537 --> 00:07:19,366
Bay, Cove, East, and Fort can all be reached.

137
00:07:19,366 --> 00:07:22,347
Dock and Gate cannot, even though they are on the map,

138
00:07:22,347 --> 00:07:24,652
because no arrow leads into them.

139
00:07:24,652 --> 00:07:27,695
Reachability is no longer symmetric.

140
00:07:28,619 --> 00:07:33,294
The came-from arrows still form a tree, pointing away from Arch.

141
00:07:33,294 --> 00:07:36,549
But it is a tree of only the stations Arch can reach,

142
00:07:36,549 --> 00:07:39,075
not a spanning tree of the whole map.

143
00:07:39,075 --> 00:07:43,238
Stacks, queues, and priority queues all still work.

144
00:07:44,107 --> 00:07:45,539
One more use.

145
00:07:45,539 --> 00:07:47,948
The paint bucket in a drawing program.

146
00:07:47,948 --> 00:07:53,508
Each pixel is a vertex, and two neighboring
pixels with the same color share an edge.

147
00:07:53,508 --> 00:07:56,386
Those edges form a graph on the pixels.

148
00:07:56,386 --> 00:08:01,151
Click a pixel, and the bucket must recolor everything reachable from it.

149
00:08:01,151 --> 00:08:03,296
That is reachability again.

150
00:08:04,244 --> 00:08:09,687
So we run a search from the clicked pixel, and
recolor each pixel the moment it is marked.

151
00:08:09,687 --> 00:08:15,403
The other blob of the same color is untouched,
because no chain of same-colored neighbors

152
00:08:15,403 --> 00:08:16,506
leads to it.

153
00:08:17,402 --> 00:08:19,329
This is called a reduction.

154
00:08:19,329 --> 00:08:23,926
We solve the paint bucket problem by calling reachability as a black box.

155
00:08:23,926 --> 00:08:30,032
A grid of n by n pixels has n squared
vertices, and at most two n squared edges,

156
00:08:30,032 --> 00:08:32,897
so the search runs in n squared time.

157
00:08:32,897 --> 00:08:36,100
All that matters about the search is what it promises,

158
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:37,555
not how it works.

159
00:08:38,475 --> 00:08:39,842
Let us retell it.

160
00:08:39,842 --> 00:08:44,973
About two E plus one tokens, so a stack or queue search is linear.

161
00:08:44,973 --> 00:08:47,824
Swap the pile, and you swap the search.

162
00:08:47,824 --> 00:08:51,874
Loop over every station, to cover every network.

163
00:08:51,874 --> 00:08:55,051
Arrows change who can be reached, not the code.

164
00:08:55,051 --> 00:08:58,289
Next time: counting paths, from class.
